Acknowledgements — ideas this project learned from
==================================================

persona-stream is not the first system to give agents memory, and it does not
claim to have invented the ideas it builds on. What it contributes is one
specific point in the design space: a *file-level, auditable, biographical*
persona distilled from a user's real working behavior, with privacy boundaries
enforced by engineering rather than promises. The lineage:

- **Zettelkasten-style agentic memory — A-MEM** (Xu et al., 2025,
  arXiv:2502.12110): atomic notes with LLM-generated structure, dynamic link
  generation between notes, and memory evolution. Our thread `related: [...]`
  cross-references (with dead-link and self-reference checks in
  `eval/audit_persona.py`) are a file-level, validity-checked simplification
  of A-MEM's link graph.

- **Bi-temporal knowledge graphs with edge invalidation — Zep / Graphiti**
  (Rasmussen et al., 2025, arXiv:2501.13956): facts carry valid-at and
  invalid-at timestamps; contradictions soft-invalidate instead of deleting.
  Our `invalidated:` fields on policy rules, `〔已失效 …〕` markers on thread
  blocks, and append-only belief version chains are the same principle
  applied to plain YAML files instead of a graph database.

- **Recency × importance × relevance retrieval — Generative Agents**
  (Park et al., 2023): memory retrieval as a scored, budgeted composition.
  Our SessionStart injection is cruder — recency (last_seen) ordering plus
  cwd-targeted recall under hard character budgets — but the same instinct:
  injection is a frozen snapshot with an explicit budget, not a dump.

- **Forgetting curves for agents — MemoryBank** (Zhong et al., 2023):
  memories fade on a schedule unless reinforced. Our four-level thread
  lifecycle (open → dormant → closed → archived, enforced mechanically by
  `distiller/foldlib.py sweep`) is the same idea with the memory *never*
  deleted — dormancy and archival instead of decay.

- **File-based memory is a serious baseline — Letta (MemGPT)**: Letta's 2025
  benchmark post ("Is a Filesystem All You Need?") showed plain files scoring
  74.0% on LoCoMo, ahead of many specialized memory systems. This result is
  part of why persona-stream commits to FTS5 + YAML with no embedding or
  graph-database dependency.

- **Time-aware memory operations — Mem0**: anchored, timestamped memory
  operations and the ongoing discussion of memory decay (a hosted-tier
  feature there; absent from most open-source editions) informed our choice
  to make forgetting a first-class, mechanically-enforced lifecycle.

The "biographical persona" framing — distilling a person's *policies,
beliefs, open threads and protocols* (not chat facts) from long-running
collaboration — is the project's own synthesis, as is the three-gate QA
discipline and the single-controlled-write-path rule. Thanks to the authors
above for making their work easy to learn from.
